{"title":"Externally tangent","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>Two <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Smooth&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Smooth (page does not exist)\">smooth</a> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Closed_curve&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Closed curve (page does not exist)\">closed curves</a> are said to be <b>externally tangent</b> if they are tangent and each lies on the exterior of the other.  Equivalently, they <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Intersect\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Intersect\">intersect</a> at a <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Point\" title=\"Point\">point</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, at this point they have a common <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Tangent_line\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Tangent line\">tangent line</a>, and locally they lie on different sides of this line.\n</p><p>[quote]\n</p>\n<ul><li><ul><li>Externally Tangent Circles**</li></ul></li></ul>\n<p>Two circles are said to be externally tangent if they touch each other at exactly one point. Let the two circles have centers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/6/1e687f9013f5cc342750d7dba1086aed655178ca.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[O_1\\]\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/3/183e7b3ba78204e030ee41b4a0dbbaafe38d47b4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[O_2\\]\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> and radii <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/8/dc89c014696b3044e825135583ce7d18dc3795f2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[r_1\\]\" width=\"13\" height=\"11\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/0/4404075a44e18c7b985e8ae465b4aeefb7305907.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[r_2\\]\" width=\"13\" height=\"11\" />. The circles are externally tangent if the distance between the centers equals the sum of the radii, that is, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/b/c0bb30fbb9cf0022052be807d9e2bf420a8be3eb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[O_1O_2 = r_1 + r_2\\]\" width=\"118\" height=\"15\" />. The point where the circles meet is called the point of tangency. At this point, the line joining the centers passes directly through it. Externally tangent circles do not overlap, unlike internally tangent circles, where one lies inside the other. \n</p><p>Consider an example where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/8/85878c87f15714173b9d0ae4c9b0ce4cb25b41ff.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[r_1 = 3\\]\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/2/e224fe63566e434d5ee129f3fe860020d1473a5f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[r_2 = 5\\]\" width=\"48\" height=\"16\" />. Then the distance between centers is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/7/df746cbf38992db4d711cfc39b23f0a7f58e405e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[O_1O_2 = 3 + 5 = 8\\]\" width=\"139\" height=\"16\" />. The point of tangency divides the segment joining the centers in the ratio of the radii, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/3/f43a1b2e3e1ed8015489222243a21067ff6ca436.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[O_1P : O_2P = r_1 : r_2\\]\" width=\"154\" height=\"15\" />. If we place <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/6/1e687f9013f5cc342750d7dba1086aed655178ca.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[O_1\\]\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/2/c92d15aa4c57ad97d64b6b40d36e65864cc6829f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(0,0)\\]\" width=\"37\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/3/183e7b3ba78204e030ee41b4a0dbbaafe38d47b4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[O_2\\]\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/f/4bfd389b6b63c77bb60e88ffa1b5d9d8de5036fd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(x,0)\\]\" width=\"38\" height=\"18\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/5/7e584b93ba9bac30b6ab1e7114ef7fb003528df4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x = r_1 + r_2\\]\" width=\"86\" height=\"13\" />. The point of tangency <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/3/c63e39cf43a302c3745a5baefefff5192aae936f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P\\]\" width=\"13\" height=\"12\" /> has coordinates <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/b/fcb994423e25e76868ad31504ee7b8357f068173.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P = \\left(\\frac{r_1x}{r_1+r_2},0\\right)\\]\" width=\"135\" height=\"43\" />. Substituting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/5/7e584b93ba9bac30b6ab1e7114ef7fb003528df4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x = r_1 + r_2\\]\" width=\"86\" height=\"13\" /> gives <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/2/b/d2b8c481f13d77a121ec7af97ac733fbc1dbac90.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P = (r_1,0)\\]\" width=\"83\" height=\"18\" />. This confirms that the point lies on the circle of radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/8/dc89c014696b3044e825135583ce7d18dc3795f2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[r_1\\]\" width=\"13\" height=\"11\" />. \n</p><p>Externally tangent circles are useful in many geometric constructions, such as Apollonius circle problems. They also appear in problems involving common external tangents. A line tangent to both circles is called a common external tangent, and there are typically two such tangents. These tangents do not intersect the line joining the centers. In coordinate geometry, the equations of two circles can be written as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/7/917e8063e608c7d83bd345e114da6a955adca1d2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(x-a_1)^2 + (y-b_1)^2 = r_1^2\\]\" width=\"199\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906349ddfe5e379bda243c3d33c4cb7d69a8adca.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(x-a_2)^2 + (y-b_2)^2 = r_2^2\\]\" width=\"199\" height=\"20\" />. The slope <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/5/2f5fbe53ed6a83c1a90f6ecb67b768ca78cc3af7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[m\\]\" width=\"15\" height=\"8\" /> of the external tangent satisfies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/4/ef47a15d0a3ac9902eb691d05099cba09dc08d98.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{|(b_2-b_1) - m(a_2-a_1)|}{\\sqrt{1+m^2}} = r_1 + r_2\\]\" width=\"264\" height=\"43\" />, derived from the distance formula. \n</p><p>Externally tangent circles also appear in packing problems, such as circle packing in rectangles. If three circles are mutually externally tangent, they form a triad, with the centers forming a triangle. The sides of the triangle equal the sum of corresponding radii. This concept is used in Descartes’ Circle Theorem. Constructing externally tangent circles is straightforward: place a compass at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/6/1e687f9013f5cc342750d7dba1086aed655178ca.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[O_1\\]\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> to draw the first circle, then place it at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/3/183e7b3ba78204e030ee41b4a0dbbaafe38d47b4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[O_2\\]\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> with radius <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/0/4404075a44e18c7b985e8ae465b4aeefb7305907.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[r_2\\]\" width=\"13\" height=\"11\" />, adjust so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/b/c0bb30fbb9cf0022052be807d9e2bf420a8be3eb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[O_1O_2 = r_1 + r_2\\]\" width=\"118\" height=\"15\" />, and draw the second circle. The circles will touch externally at exactly one point. \n</p><p>These circles also model real-world situations. In chains of gears, each gear touches the next externally. In physics, circular objects in contact can be approximated as externally tangent circles. Understanding external tangency simplifies calculations in coordinate geometry and helps in algebraic problems involving distances. Externally tangent circles connect concepts in geometry, algebra, and real-world applications, forming a fundamental idea in mathematics. \n[/quote]\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018010426\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.011 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.021 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 85/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 4552/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:4898:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018010426 and revision id 280832. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}